{"text": "Voluntary wheel running restores hippocampal BDNF signalling and spatial memory in aged mice[SEP]Brain-derived neurotrophic factor (BDNF) supports synaptic plasticity, and its expression in the hippocampus declines with age. Whether aerobic exercise late in life can reverse this decline, and whether any recovery depends on TrkB signalling, remains unclear. We housed 20-month-old male and female C57BL/6 mice with or without access to a running wheel for six weeks (n = 24 per group) and compared them with 4-month-old controls. Running increased hippocampal BDNF protein by 38% relative to sedentary aged mice and restored levels of phosphorylated TrkB to those of young animals. In the Morris water maze, running mice located the hidden platform faster from day three of training and spent more time in the target quadrant during the probe trial. Dendritic spine density on CA1 pyramidal neurons rose by 21%, and long-term potentiation at Schaffer collateral synapses was enhanced. Blocking TrkB with ANA-12 during the final two weeks abolished the gains in spine density and memory without changing running distance, indicating that the benefits require BDNF-TrkB signalling rather than general activity. Effects were similar in males and females. Serum BDNF did not correlate with hippocampal levels, which cautions against using circulating BDNF as a proxy for brain changes in exercise trials. These results suggest that the aged hippocampus retains the capacity for exercise-induced plasticity and identify TrkB activation as a necessary step. Because the intervention began at an age equivalent to roughly 65 human years, the findings support testing structured aerobic training in older adults with mild cognitive complaints, with outcome measures that do not rely on blood biomarkers alone.", "fingerprint_fp32": [0.442341685295105, 0.015039488673210144, -0.31626901030540466, 0.05297764763236046, -0.4521457552909851, 0.5887773036956787, -0.3620685935020447, -0.956004023551940